        exp: "Nobody warns foreign residents about this one, and it will arrive within a month of your moving in.\n\nA neighbour knocks and hands you a flat folder, often plastic, with a grid of names printed down the front and an empty box beside each. Inside is a single A4 sheet about refuse collection, or a fire drill, or the summer festival. This is a 回覧板 — a circulating noticeboard — and the arrangement is that you read the sheet, tick or stamp the box beside your name, and carry the folder to the next house on the list.\n\nThat is the entire obligation, and getting it wrong is one of the more common quiet embarrassments of a first year: the folder sitting on a table for a week while eleven households wait for it.\n\nWhat makes it worth a lesson here is what is printed on the sheet. A neighbourhood notice is built almost entirely out of the particles this step just taught you.\n\n「ゴミの分別について」 — concerning refuse separation. 「工事に関するお知らせ」 — notice regarding construction. 「大雨による影響」 — the effects caused by heavy rain. 「本会館において」 — at this community hall. Six compound particles will get you through most of a ward newsletter, and their absence is the single reason those sheets look impenetrable at N4 and turn legible almost overnight once you have them.\n\nNotice also what these headings do NOT have: verbs. A Japanese notice heading hangs a noun off a relation and stops, which is a shape you will meet again in Step 36 with newspaper headlines.\n\nThe folder is on its way out, slowly. Tokyo's metropolitan government now subsidises 町会・自治会 that move to 電子回覧板 — an electronic version, usually a messaging group or an app — and wards including Minato run their own digital schemes. It is not a clean replacement. The people who actually run a 町内会 skew elderly, so most areas that digitise end up running paper and digital side by side, which doubles the work for the volunteers doing it.\n\nSo the folder will probably still knock. Read it, stamp it, pass it on.",
