        exp: "A Japanese news headline is not a short sentence. It is a different grammar, and knowing the rules turns a wall of kanji into something you can skim.\n\n**The verb is usually gone.** 「首相、来月訪米」 — Prime Minister, next month, visit-America. No verb, no particles, and a comma doing the work of は. Headlines are built from nouns pressed together, which is why they look dense and read fast once you expect it.\n\n**へ marks the future.** 「値上げへ」 means a price rise is coming. Where a sentence would need ことになりました, a headline uses a single particle and stops.\n\n**か marks the unconfirmed.** 「不正か」 — possible wrongdoing. The paper is telling you it does not yet know, and か carries the whole hedge.\n\n**と carries quotes and findings.** 「３割が『知らない』と回答」 — thirty per cent answered that they did not know. This is where the reporting grammar from this step lives, and 〜によると in the body of the article is the same move at full length: name the source, then report.\n\n**Numbers and 割.** Japanese reports proportions in 割 — tenths — far more readily than in percentages. ３割 is thirty per cent, and it will appear that way in the headline and often in the first paragraph.\n\nThe reason to learn this at exactly this step is that a headline is the highest-density place where 〜によると, 〜に比べて and 〜につれて all appear inside forty characters. 「調査によると、去年に比べて３割減」 — according to the survey, a thirty per cent fall compared with last year — is a complete news story in nineteen characters, and every relation in it is one you learned this step.\n\nA practical note: NHK publishes 「NEWS WEB EASY」, a version of its news written for children and for learners, with furigana and simplified grammar. It is the single best free reading resource at this level, and it uses these same headline conventions in a gentler form.",
