        exp: {
          what: "The formal circumstance-and-purpose set. とあって reports a special circumstance and its natural consequence: 連休とあって、どこも混んでいた — it being the holidays, everywhere was packed. とあれば accepts a condition as sufficient cause: 君の頼みとあれば、断れない — if it's you asking, refusal is off the table. ゆえに gives reason in scholar's ink: 我思う、ゆえに我あり. んがため（に） is purpose at maximum formality, the ん of んばかり serving desire: 勝たんがために、手段を選ばなかった — for the sake of winning, no means were beneath them.",
          build: "Noun／plain clause + とあって + observed consequence (third-person, reported). Noun／と + あれば. Noun／plain + （が）ゆえ（に）. ない-stem + んがため（に） (する → せんがため).",
          when: "とあって is news-Japanese's favorite scene-setter — every holiday-crowd report opens with it. とあれば in loyal pledges; ゆえに in logic and law; んがために in editorials describing single-minded pursuit, usually with an eyebrow raised.",
          watch: "とあって needs a genuinely special circumstance and cannot take the speaker's own situation. んがため's purpose is usually judged excessive by the sentence around it — the pattern itself carries the eyebrow. And ゆえに outside logic, law or deliberate gravity reads as cosplay.",
        },
        ex: [["三連休の初日とあって、高速道路は朝から渋滞した。", "It being the first day of the long weekend, the expressways jammed from morning."], ["生き残らんがために、会社は何でも切り捨てた。", "For the sake of survival, the company cut away everything."]],
